  "id": "naayeri_mitote_judea",
  "name": "Náayeri mitote and Judea (Cora ceremonial music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Ceremonial music of the Náayeri (Cora) of the Sierra del Nayar in Nayarit. At the mitote, the night dance of the agricultural cycle held around a fire, a singer chants while striking a large musical bow that rests on a gourd resonator; at Holy Week in Jesús María the Judea, with its painted dancers, is accompanied by cane flute and drum. Konrad Theodor Preuss recorded Cora songs on phonograph cylinders in 1905-1907 for the Berlin Phonogramm-Archiv. The ugubhu card, a gourd-resonated struck bow, stands in for the mitote bow, and generic flute and drum cards for the Judea flute and drum.",
  "recipe": "classic 1905-1907 náayeri mitote and judea, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. ugubhu, flute like credited flute unspecified, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
